  "notes": [
    "This is retrospective mechanism diagnosis. It cannot promote a policy. A follow-up centered-advantage smoke is justified only if all six strata have sufficient support and global normalization leaves material nonzero stratum mean advantage or disproportionate signed gradient mass that centering removes.",
    "The preregistered hypothesis included high-variance partition dominance. Initial run quantified group means and exposed a 5.83x decision-count range but did not serialize absolute/squared advantage mass. Same immutable inputs are re-aggregated in run_variance; no result selection or weight update."
  ],
  "result": {
    "summary": "Reconstructed all 8,925 zero-residual decisions from 4,096 saved training hands and joined aggregate untouched evaluation. Six opponent-seat groups had nearly zero normalized mean offsets, but decision counts differed 5.83x and advantage standard deviations 3.05x. Slumbot-free seat1 alone contributed 39.87% of squared advantage mass despite 14.51% of decisions.",
    "conclusion": "Global mean centering was not the defect. Heteroskedastic opponent-seat returns caused one Slumbot-free seat stratum to dominate PPO gradient energy, matching the only anchor that improved. This supplies a concrete explanation for narrow specialization and a bounded intervention: groupwise RMS scaling on a new common dataset.",
    "decision": "ADMIT_GROUP_RMS_ADVANTAGE_MATCHED_SMOKE",
    "next_step": "Run one matched offline-update experiment from identical zero residuals on a new fixed 4,096-hand dataset: global-normalized control versus opponent-seat centered/RMS-scaled treatment, identical minibatches and residual cap, then untouched common-deck evaluation. Do not use prior evaluation decks or Slumbot."
  },
